@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Clustered Index";
}

<div class="container-fluid">
    <div class="row">

        <!-- =========================
             MAIN CONTENT
        ========================== -->

        <div class="col-lg-12">

            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">

                <div class="card-body p-4">

                    <!-- =========================
                         TITLE
                    ========================== -->

                    <h1 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                        Clustered Index
                    </h1>


                    <!-- =========================
                         INTRODUCTION
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
                        What is a Clustered Index?
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A <strong>Clustered Index</strong> determines the physical
                        order in which data rows are stored in a table.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The data rows of the table are stored according to the
                        clustered index key. Because the actual table data is
                        arranged according to the clustered index, a table can
                        have only <strong>one clustered index</strong>.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         HOW CLUSTERED INDEX WORKS
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        How Does a Clustered Index Work?
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Suppose we have a Students table containing the following
                        records:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>StudentId    StudentName    City
--------------------------------
103          Rahul          Pune
101          Amit           Mumbai
105          Neha           Pune
102          Priya          Delhi
104          Rohit          Nashik</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        If we create a clustered index on
                        <strong>StudentId</strong>, SQL Server organizes the
                        table data according to the StudentId values.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>StudentId    StudentName    City
--------------------------------
101          Amit           Mumbai
102          Priya          Delhi
103          Rahul          Pune
104          Rohit          Nashik
105          Neha           Pune</code></pre>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Creating a Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A clustered index can be created using the
                        <strong>CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX</strong> statement.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_StudentId
ON Students(StudentId);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Here:
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>IX_Students_StudentId</strong> is the index name.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Students</strong> is the table name.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>StudentId</strong> is the indexed column.
                        </li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         PRIMARY KEY AND CLUSTERED
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Clustered Index with Primary Key
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        When a primary key is created, SQL Server generally
                        creates a clustered index for the primary key by default,
                        unless another index option is specified.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        In this example, the primary key on
                        <strong>StudentId</strong> can have a clustered index
                        associated with it.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         ONE CLUSTERED INDEX
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Only One Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A table can have only one clustered index because the
                        actual data rows can be physically ordered in only one
                        way.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For example, we can create a clustered index on
                        <strong>StudentId</strong>:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_StudentId
ON Students(StudentId);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        We cannot create another clustered index on the same table
                        while the existing clustered index is present.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         QUERY EXAMPLE
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Example Query
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Consider the following query:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 103;</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        If StudentId is the clustered index key, SQL Server can
                        efficiently locate the required row using the index
                        structure.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CLUSTERED INDEX ADVANTAGES
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Advantages of Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            Improves data retrieval performance.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Data is physically organized according to the
                            clustered index key.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Useful for range-based queries.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Can improve ORDER BY operations on the indexed key.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Useful when records are frequently searched using
                            the indexed column.
                        </li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         DISADVANTAGES
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Disadvantages of Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            A table can have only one clustered index.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Insert and update operations may require data
                            reorganization.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Creating and maintaining indexes requires additional
                            database resources.
                        </li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CLUSTERED INDEX EXAMPLE
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Complete Example
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Create a Students table:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Insert sample records:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES
(103, 'Rahul', 'Pune', 22),
(101, 'Amit', 'Mumbai', 21),
(105, 'Neha', 'Pune', 23),
(102, 'Priya', 'Delhi', 22),
(104, 'Rohit', 'Nashik', 24);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Create a clustered index:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_StudentId
ON Students(StudentId);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Now SQL Server can organize the table data according to
                        StudentId.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CLUSTERED INDEX VS NORMAL
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-info mt-5">

                        <h4 class="fw-bold">
                            Important
                        </h4>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            A clustered index is closely related to the physical
                            organization of table data. Since the actual data
                            rows can have only one physical order, a table can
                            have only one clustered index.
                        </p>

                    </div>


                    <!-- =========================
                         IMPORTANT POINTS
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-warning mt-4">

                        <h4 class="fw-bold">
                            Important Points
                        </h4>

                        <ul class="mb-0">

                            <li>
                                A clustered index determines the physical order
                                of data rows.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                A table can have only one clustered index.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                Clustered indexes are useful for fast data
                                retrieval.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                Primary keys are commonly associated with
                                clustered indexes by default.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                Clustered indexes are useful for range queries.
                            </li>

                        </ul>

                    </div>


                    <!-- =========================
                         SUMMARY
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-primary mt-5">

                        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                            Summary
                        </h2>

                        <p>
                            A <strong>Clustered Index</strong> determines the
                            physical order of data rows in a SQL Server table.
                            The data is organized according to the clustered
                            index key.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            A table can have only one clustered index because
                            the table data can have only one physical order.
                        </p>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Clustered indexes are useful for improving data
                            retrieval performance, especially when searching
                            and retrieving data using the indexed key.
                        </p>

                    </div>


